Kimigayo: ignore LD_LIBRARY_PATH and LD_PRELOAD from the environment

Why: OpenRC (and anything else dynamically linked against musl) could be
code-injected by anyone able to set an environment variable for it.
Upstream musl only ignores these for suid/sgid processes; in a container
almost everything is root without suid, so that guard never fires.

Measured on the published v3.0.1 image before this patch:
  LD_PRELOAD=/root/evil.so /sbin/openrc --version
  -> "*** INJECTED via LD_PRELOAD ***" printed by the injected library

The statically linked BusyBox was immune only because it does not use the
dynamic loader at all. This patch closes the hole for every dynamic binary,
which is what makes a dynamically linked BusyBox acceptable.

Applied by scripts/apply-musl-patches.sh. If it stops applying after a musl
version bump, the build FAILS on purpose: a security patch that silently
does not apply is worse than no patch (see CLAUDE.md on
apply-kernel-patches.sh skipping patches).

--- a/ldso/dynlink.c
+++ b/ldso/dynlink.c
@@ -1817,11 +1817,20 @@
 	libc.secure = ((aux[0]&0x7800)!=0x7800 || aux[AT_UID]!=aux[AT_EUID]
 		|| aux[AT_GID]!=aux[AT_EGID] || aux[AT_SECURE]);
 
-	/* Only trust user/env if kernel says we're not suid/sgid */
-	if (!libc.secure) {
-		env_path = getenv("LD_LIBRARY_PATH");
-		env_preload = getenv("LD_PRELOAD");
-	}
+	/* Kimigayo: never take LD_LIBRARY_PATH or LD_PRELOAD from the
+	 * environment, regardless of libc.secure.
+	 *
+	 * Upstream only ignores them for suid/sgid processes. In a container
+	 * nearly everything runs as root without suid, so anyone who can set
+	 * an environment variable for a process (a service file, an exec'd
+	 * child, a crafted `docker exec -e`) can inject code into every
+	 * dynamically linked binary - including the init system.
+	 *
+	 * Library search paths that the system genuinely needs belong in
+	 * /etc/ld-musl-$ARCH.path (root-owned file, not caller-controlled).
+	 * Explicitly running the loader (`ld-musl-*.so.1 --preload X prog`)
+	 * still works; that requires the ability to exec arbitrary programs
+	 * already, so it grants nothing new. */
 
 	/* Activate error handler function */
 	error = error_impl;
